<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean Parisot de Valette</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1495">1495</a>–<a href="/years/1568">1568</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Jean de Valette</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean Parisot de Valette was a French knight who was Grand Master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> from 1557 to 1568. He led the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> during the Great Siege of 1565, when the knights and Maltese held out for four months against a large Ottoman army until a relief force arrived. Afterwards he founded the new fortified capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valletta">Valletta</a>, named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-parisot-de-valette.jpg" alt="Jean Parisot de Valette" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean-parisot-de-la-valette.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jean Parisot de Valette was born into the noble La Valette family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quercy">Quercy</a>, in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, a family that had been important for many generations, various members having taken part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a>. His grandfather Bernard de La Valette was a knight and king's orderly, and his father Guillot was a Chevalier de France. He joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> in the Langue de Provence at about the age of 20, around 1514, and never returned to France or his family estates. He was present at the Great Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> in 1522 and accompanied Grand Master Philippe Villiers de L'Isle-Adam after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> under Suleiman expelled the Order from Rhodes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> the Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> granted the Order the Maltese Islands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a>. In 1538 Valette was imprisoned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gozo">Gozo</a> for four months after attacking a man. In 1541 he was wounded in a naval battle against Abd-ur-Rahman Kust Aly, and his galley, the San Giovanni, was captured. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turgut-reis">Turgut Reis</a> held him as a galley slave for a year until a prisoner exchange freed him. He became Governor of Tripoli in 1546 and Captain General of the Order's galleys in 1554.</p><p>On the death of Grand Master Claude de la Sengle in 1557 the knights, aware that an attack was certain, elected Valette in his place. In 1560 he allied with the Habsburg Empire to reconquer Tripoli, but the expedition ended in a Christian defeat at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djerba">Djerba</a>. Later in his term he greatly strengthened the Order's navy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the Great Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> in 1565, Valette organised the defence against an Ottoman force of no fewer than 30,000 soldiers, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a>, with a fleet of some 193 ships. The vastly outnumbered Christians lost Fort St Elmo after about a month of fierce fighting, though the Ottoman engineers had judged that it would fall in three days. The Order held out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birgu">Birgu</a> and Senglea until a relief force arrived, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> withdrew with too few men, supplies and too little morale to counterattack. Valette gained great prestige in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and declined the offer of a cardinal's hat to preserve independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>.</p><p>In 1566 he laid with his own hands the first stone of a new city on Mount Sciberras, named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valletta">Valletta</a> after its founder. He suffered a stroke while praying in a chapel and died on 21 August 1568, exactly eleven years after his election, before the city was complete. He was succeeded by Pierre de Monte, and his tomb lies in the crypt of the Conventual Church, now St John's Co-Cathedral. The form of his surname is disputed: the historian Giovanni Bonello states that he always signed himself de Valette, without the La.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jean Parisot de Valette” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After nearly four months, the Ottoman army withdrew from Malta, defeated by the Knights of St John under <b>Jean de Valette</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-8">8 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1565 <b>Jean de Valette</b> defended Malta against the Ottoman Great Siege, and the new capital Valletta was built from 1566.</blockquote><a href="/state/hospitaller-malta">Hospitaller Malta</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Parisot, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Malta. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.27,1.86&amp;year=1542" aria-label="Parisot on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jean Parisot de Valette"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5114 3792h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5114 3792h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.27,1.86&amp;year=1542">What was here in 1542? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
